{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-347050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/eda6639ced5b182d7a05017883cf15dfbc1ce4b11e4ad3161d0ed6f9d2ab982c.png)\n\nEstimate the area $A$ under the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+9x$ over the interval $[0,9]$ using the trapezoidal rule with $3$ trapezoids of equal height. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-347050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+9x$ and let $A$ denote the required area. The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(a)+2(f({x}_{1})+f({x}_{2})+…+f({x}_{n-1}))+f(b)]Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=3.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[0,9],$ so we have $a=0$ and $b=9.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{9-0}{3}=3.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(0)+2(f(3)+f(6))+f(9)]\\cdot 3.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $x$ and $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0$ | $18$ | $18$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [0+2(18+18)+0]\\cdot 3=108\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave down* over the interval $[0,9],$ the trapezoids lie under the function, and therefore the approximation is an *underestimate* of the correct area." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-347050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4b62787f769c79c89ecea6de9ba458f172c4ff5cad3ecb64410e86ddb5e889cb"} {"question" "q-347050" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-347050" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "108"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-364578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/732c560cfab8b78b2f37ae6623567be2ec2934b34d9b5b40d1c9bebcf0f9e92c.png)\n\nEstimate the area $A$ under the curve $y={x}^{2}+2$ over the interval $[0,6]$ using the trapezoidal rule with $3$ trapezoids of equal height. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-364578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}+2$ and let $A$ denote the required area. The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(a)+2(f({x}_{1})+f({x}_{2})+…+f({x}_{n-1}))+f(b)]Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=3.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[0,6],$ so we have $a=0$ and $b=6.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{6-0}{3}=2.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(0)+2(f(2)+f(4))+f(6)]\\cdot 2.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $x$ and $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $6$ | $18$ | $38$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [2+2(6+18)+38]\\cdot 2=88\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave up* over the interval $[0,6],$ the trapezoids lie over the function, and therefore the approximation is an *overestimate* of the correct area." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-364578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4b62787f769c79c89ecea6de9ba458f172c4ff5cad3ecb64410e86ddb5e889cb"} {"question" "q-364578" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-364578" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "88"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-364580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/24fd0ccf835405c3da099e0993a3cebe6711ecd2f0c4c1e960275be3dad032aa.png)\n\nEstimate the area under the curve $y=\\frac{120}{{x}^{2}-1}$ over the interval $[2,4]$ using the trapezoidal rule with $2$ trapezoids of equal height. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-364580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(x)=\\frac{120}{{x}^{2}-1}$ and let $A$ denote the required area. The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(a)+2(f({x}_{1})+f({x}_{2})+…+f({x}_{n-1}))+f(b)]Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=2.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[2,4],$ so we have $a=2$ and $b=4.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{4-2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(2)+2f(3)+f(4)]\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $x$ and $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $40$ | $15$ | $8$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [40+2(15)+8]\\cdot 1=39\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave up* over the interval $[2,4],$ the trapezoids lie over the function, and therefore approximation is an *overestimate* of the correct area." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-364580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4b62787f769c79c89ecea6de9ba458f172c4ff5cad3ecb64410e86ddb5e889cb"} {"question" "q-364580" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-364580" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "39"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-85637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/0c5c97425f6a03a55067f8848b7bae1c20d2db3e924bf9aaa277c99c34c65364.png)\n\nEstimate the area under the curve $y=1-{x}^{3}$ over the interval $[-2,0]$ using the trapezoidal rule with $2$ trapezoids of equal height.\n\n*Note: figure not drawn to scale.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-85637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(x)=1-{x}^{3}$ and let $A$ denote the required area. The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(a)+2(f({x}_{1})+f({x}_{2})+…+f({x}_{n-1}))+f(b)]Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=2.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[-2,0],$ so we have $a=-2$ and $b=0.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{0-(-2)}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(-2)+2f(-1)+f(0)]\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $x$ and $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $9$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[9+2(2)+1]\\cdot 1=7\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave up* over the interval $[-2,0],$ the trapezoids lie over the function, and therefore approximation is an *overestimate* of the correct area." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-85637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4b62787f769c79c89ecea6de9ba458f172c4ff5cad3ecb64410e86ddb5e889cb"} {"question" "q-85637" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-85637" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "6"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "\\frac{15}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{29}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-85637" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-85636" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/89426d77a95ffb1a4780484ed1e1ad0531ccd1c4022419ef2c573a08922bf7eb.png)\n\nEstimate the area under the curve $y=4-{x}^{2}$ over the interval $[-1,2]$ using the trapezoidal rule with $3$ trapezoids of equal height.\n\n*Note: figure not drawn to scale.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-85636" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(x)=4-{x}^{2}$ and let $A$ denote the required area. The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(a)+2(f({x}_{1})+f({x}_{2})+…+f({x}_{n-1}))+f(b)]Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=3.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[-1,2],$ so we have $a=-1$ and $b=2.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{2-(-1)}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(-1)+2(f(0)+f(1))+f(2)]\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $x$ and $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $4$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[3+2(4+3)+0]\\cdot 1=\\frac{17}{2}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave down* over the interval $[-1,2],$ the trapezoids lie under the function, and therefore approximation is an *underestimate* of the correct area." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-85636" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4b62787f769c79c89ecea6de9ba458f172c4ff5cad3ecb64410e86ddb5e889cb"} {"question" "q-85636" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-85636" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "\\frac{17}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{21}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{25}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-85636" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{17}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-116081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Fo