2b281338ce0fc7d64677b260f6c0d8cdaa47855afe35bae0c21fde750a39d5.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overset{1\\text{st trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(4)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(4)+f(7)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{3\\text{rd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(7)+f(15)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{3}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{1+5}{2}\\cdot (4-0)+\\frac{5+6}{2}\\cdot (7-4)+\\frac{6+2}{2}\\cdot (15-7) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4+\\frac{11}{2}\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =12+\\frac{33}{2}+32 \\\\  & =\\frac{121}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-347099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4b62787f769c79c89ecea6de9ba458f172c4ff5cad3ecb64410e86ddb5e889cb"} {"question" "q-347099" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-347099" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{121}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-85726" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Using the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[-3,3]=[-3,-2]\\cup [-2,0]\\cup [0,3],\n$$\n\napproximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $6$ | $4$ | $3$ | $1$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-85726" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/3a9a6a7a340299c0b1f3fa349ad705c34079b74b0d3d1d1c3205d12ed9ef485d.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overset{1\\text{st trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-3)+f(-2)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-2)+f(0)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{3\\text{rd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(3)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{3}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{6+4}{2}\\cdot (-2-(-3))+\\frac{4+3}{2}\\cdot (0-(-2))+\\frac{3+1}{2}\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1+\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =5+7+6 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-85726" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4b62787f769c79c89ecea6de9ba458f172c4ff5cad3ecb64410e86ddb5e889cb"} {"question" "q-85726" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-85726" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "14"] ["math" "18"] ["math" "22"] ["math" "\\frac{33}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{39}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-85726" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "18"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-49238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The rate, in degrees Farhenheit per month, at which the average midday temperature in New York City varies, does so according to the function $R(t)={t}^{3}+3,$ where $t$ is the number of months since the measurements began. Estimate the total increase in air temperature over the first three months using the trapezoidal rule with $3$ trapezoids of equal height." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-49238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In this context, the area $A$ under the curve $y=R(t)$ over the interval $[0,3]$ represents the total increase in air temperature over the first three months.\n\nThe general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[R(a)+2(R({t}_{1})+R({t}_{2})+⋯+R({t}_{n-1}))+R(b)]Δt,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δt=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=3.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[0,3],$ so we have $a=0$ and $b=3.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{3-0}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[R(0)+2(R(1)+R(2))+R(3)]\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $t$ and $R(t):$\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)$ | $3$ | $4$ | $11$ | $30$ |\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{1}{2}[3+2(4+11)+30]\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{2} \\\\  & =31.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total air temperature increase is approximately ${31.5}^{∘}\\,\\text{F}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-49238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4b62787f769c79c89ecea6de9ba458f172c4ff5cad3ecb64410e86ddb5e889cb"} {"question" "q-49238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-49238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{24.5}^{∘}\\,\\text{F}"] ["math" "{31.5}^{∘}\\,\\text{F}"] ["math" "{45}^{∘}\\,\\text{F}"] ["math" "{48.5}^{∘}\\,\\text{F}"] ["math" "{50}^{∘}\\,\\text{F}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-49238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{31.5}^{∘}\\,\\text{F}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-116618" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Lisa is selling orange juice. The following table shows the rate, in liters per hour, at which it is sold.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hour})$ | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $c(t)\\,(\\text{lts/hour})$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n\nUse the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[1,2]\\cup [2,5],\n$$\n\nto approximate the total number of liters of juice sold by Lisa over a 4 hours period." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-116618" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In this context, we have $2$ trapezoids, and the area $A$ under the curve $y=c(t)$ represents the total number of liters sold over a $4$ period.\n\nUsing the trapezoidal rule, we estimate the area as follows:\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{c(1)+c(2)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{1}+\\frac{c(2)+c(5)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{3+5}{2}\\cdot (2-1)+\\frac{5+7}{2}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =22\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Lisa sold approximately $22\\,\\text{liters}$ over a $4$ hours period." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-116618" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4b62787f769c79c89ecea6de9ba458f172c4ff5cad3ecb64410e86ddb5e889cb"} {"question" "q-116618" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-116618" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$11$ liters"] ["text" "$18$ liters"] ["text" "$22$ liters"] ["text" "$26$ liters"] ["text" "$32$ liters"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-116618" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$22$ liters"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-347170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A solar array’s power output rate (in kilowatts) is modeled by $p(t)=-2{t}^{2}+20t.$ Approximate the total number of kilowatt-hours of energy produced in the first nine hours using the trapezoidal rule with $3$ trapezoids of equal height.\n\nThe solar array produced  \n{{field-1}}\nkilowatt-hours of energy." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-347170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In this context, the area $A$ under the curve $y=p(t)$ over the interval $[0,9]$ represents the total number of kilowatt-hours of energy produced in the first nine hours.\n\nThe general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[p(a)+2(p({t}_{1})+p({t}_{2})+…+p({t}_{n-1}))+p(b)]Δt,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δt=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=3.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[0,9],$ so we have $a=0$ and $b=9.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{9-0}{3}=3.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[p(0)+2(p(3)+p(6))+p(9)]\\cdot 3.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $t$ and $p(t):$\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $p(t)$ | $0$ | $42$ | $48$ | $18$ |\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{1}{2}[0+2(42+48)+18]\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[198]\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =297.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solar array produced $297$ kilowatt-hours of energy in the first nine hours." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-347170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4b62787f769c79c89ecea6de9ba458f172c4ff5cad3ecb64410e86ddb5e889cb"} {"question" "q-347170" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-347170" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "297"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-51076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The following table shows the rate $r(t),$ in centimeters per hour, at which rain is falling during a two-hour period of a certain day.\n\n| $t\\,\\text{(hours)}$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r\\,\\text{(centimeters/hour)}$ | $3$ | $4$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n\nApproximate the total amount of rainfall during this period using the trapezoidal rule with $4$ trapezoids of equal height." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-51076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In this context, the area $A$ under the curve $y=r(t)$ over the interval $[0,2]$ represents the total amount of rainfall during the two-hour period.\n\nThe general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[r(a)+2(r({t}_{1})+r({t}_{2})+⋯+r({t}_{n-1}))+r(b)]Δt,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δt=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=4.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[0,2],$ so we have $a=0$ and $b=2.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{2-0}{4}=0.5.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[r(0)+2(r(0.5)+r(1)+r(1.5))+r(2)]\\cdot 0.5.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers from the given table in the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{1}{2}[3+2(4+3+3)+1]\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.25(24) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $6\\,\\text{cm}$ of rain fell during the given two-hour period." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-51076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4b62787f769c79c89ecea6de9ba458f172c4ff5cad3ecb64410e86ddb5e889cb"} {"question" "q-51076" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-51076" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4\\,\\text{cm}"] ["math" "5\\,\\text{cm}"] ["math" "6\\,\\text{cm}"] ["math" "7\\,\\text{cm}"] ["math" "8\\,\\text{cm}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-51076" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6\\,\\text{cm}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-51077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The following table shows the speed $V(t),$ in miles per hour, at which an airplane flies at certain times since its $2$-hour journey began.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hours})$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $V\\,(\\text{miles/hour})$ | $150$ | $250$ | $200$ |\n\nApproximate the total distance traveled by the plane using the trapezoidal rule with $2$ trapezoids of equal height.\n\n*Hint: The distance traveled is given by the area under the speed-time graph.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-51077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In this context, the area $A$ under the curve $y=V(t)$ over the interval $[0,2]$ represents the total distance traveled by the plane.\n\nThe general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[V(a)+2(V({t}_{1})+V({t}_{2})+⋯+V({t}_{n-1}))+V(b)]Δt,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δt=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=2.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[0,2],$ so we have $a=0$ and $b=2.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{2-0}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[V(0)+2V(1)+V(2)]\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers from the given table into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{1}{2}[150+2(250)+200]\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{850}{2} \\\\  & =425.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total distance traveled by the plane is approximately $425$ miles." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-51077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4b62787f769c79c89ecea6de9ba458f172c4ff5cad3ecb64410e86ddb5e889cb"} {"question" "q-51077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-51077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$300$ miles"] ["text" "$370$ miles"] ["text" "$390$ miles"] ["text" "$425$ miles"] ["text" "$480$ miles"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "q-51077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$425$ miles"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf1634a225c4c3e644f2084ccc5912363b741709dfc78e7b57d5b6634b41c2d9"} {"question" "e-398" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Estimate the area under the curve $y=18-x^2$ over the interval $[1,4]$ using the trapezoidal rule with $3$ trapezoids of equal height.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/c5f3d65642fe7a2944381ab2a888f0ae8a6f8e0defabf1ad545e6f3071f2b14b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/example-398.json" "file_sha256" "1944fa2470f7d47233d7c5da9feafb18213ffbe06aaa24262d148204ce923d34"} {"question" "e-398" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(x)= 18 - x^2$ and let $\\mathcal A$ denote the required area. The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx \\frac{1}{2}\\left[f(a)+ 2\\left(f(x_1) + f(x_2)+\\ldots + f(x_{n-1})\\right)+f(b) \\right]\\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size. \n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=3.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[1,4],$ so we have $a=1$ and $b=4.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n} = \\dfrac{4 - 1}{3} = 1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx \\frac{1}{2}\\left[f(1)+ 2\\left(f(2) + f(3)\\right) + f(4) \\right]\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $x$ and $f(x){:}$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $17$ | $14$ | $9$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\left[17 + 2\\left(14 + 9\\right)+ 2 \\right]\\cdot 1 = \\dfrac{65}{2}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave down* over the interval $[1,4],$ the trapezoids lie under the function, and therefore approximation is an *underestimate* of the correct area." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/example-398.json" "file_sha256" "1944fa2470f7d47233d7c5da9feafb18213ffbe06aaa24262d148204ce923d34"} {"question" "e-7515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the graphs shown below would the trapezoidal sum give an underestimate of the area under the curve between $x=-2$ and $x=2?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/97f48d1d5c9f54e5351a6f3453fe948959d26a36d30d74f9425bf40e4fd54413.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/example-7515.json" "file_sha256" "0049cedc5b1abf10f886c153fe01f75aac7d93e13d3a1a5f4b0e23618bcbeea2"} {"question" "e-7515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The trapezoidal rule underestimates the area under the curve if the function is concave down over the given interval.     \n\nFrom the given graphs, the only curve that is concave down on $[-2,2]$ is the one shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/2fa5a66712fede3f28f13d5daeaf4b8bc9a7e37eca6e438b16ccffed39514d50.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/example-7515.json" "file_sha256" "0049cedc5b1abf10f886c153fe01f75aac7d93e13d3a1a5f4b0e23618bcbeea2"} {"question" "e-399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Using the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[1,4.5] = [1,2] \\cup [2,4] \\cup [4,4.5],\n$$\n\napproximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $4.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/example-399.json" "file_sha256" "99dad27b3e31a3695c02d9a4b82bd79aae0c44f4a04cacc0335930b9844e5b2d"} {"question" "e-399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/975c49f3fc3bed42149ff267cf17455cd816df4297d6909d0c62072ec5688bd2.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $\\mathcal{A}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/4caf59c8cb226e10b58407abbff7096cac8113bb0193c58aa200dd4c956722e5.png)\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{1+5}{2}\\cdot (2-1)+\\frac{5+7}{2}\\cdot (4-2)+\\frac{7+9}{2}\\cdot (4.5-4) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 2+8\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =3+12+4 \\\\  & =19\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/example-399.json" "file_sha256" "99dad27b3e31a3695c02d9a4b82bd79aae0c44f4a04cacc0335930b9844e5b2d"} {"question" "e-400" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The following table shows the speed of a car, in miles per hour, at certain moments in its $2$-hour journey.\n\n| $t  \\left(\\textrm{hours}\\right)$ | $0$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v  \\left(\\textrm{miles}/\\textrm{hour}\\right)$ | $0$ | $60$ | $40$ |\n\nUse the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[0,1.5] \\cup [1.5,2],\n$$\n\nto approximate the total distance traveled during the $2$-hour journey." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/example-400.json" "file_sha256" "1a1c4130c78e4b78ab68569933b4882ddb22d884d7b0d22254f4745a40deaeef"} {"question" "e-400" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In this context, we have $n=2$ trapezoids, and the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the curve $y=v(t)$ represents the total distance traveled during the $2$-hour journey.\n\nUsing the trapezoidal rule, we estimate the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx  \\dfrac{ v(0)+v(1.5) }{2} \\cdot \\Delta t_1 + \\dfrac{ v(1.5)+v(2) }{2} \\cdot \\Delta t_2\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{0+60}{2}\\cdot (1.5-0)+\\frac{60+40}{2}\\cdot (2-1.5) \\\\  & =30\\cdot (1.5)+50\\cdot (0.5) \\\\  & =70\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total distance covered during the $2$-hour journey is approximately $70\\,\\textrm{miles}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/example-400.json" "file_sha256" "1a1c4130c78e4b78ab68569933b4882ddb22d884d7b0d22254f4745a40deaeef"}] "usage" {"e-7515" [] "e-400" [] "e-399" [] "e-398" []} "basis" 1841 "retractions" [[17592186091952 :question/problem "Estimate the area under the curve $y=18-x^2$ over the interval $[1,4]$ using the trapezoidal rule with $3$ trapezoids of equal height.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/945/Source/Images/dd04ee54b1306d80622dc7f51fc24f94.png)"] [17592186091952 :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)= 18 - x^2\n$$\n\nand let $\\mathcal A$ denote the required area. The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx \\frac{1}{2}\\left[f(a)+ 2\\left(f(x_1) + f(x_2)+\\ldots + f(x_{n-1})\\right)+f(b) \\right]\\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}\n$$\n\nis the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=3$.\n- The corresponding interval is $[1,4]$, so we have $a=1$ and $b=4$.\n- The step size is given by\n$\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n} = \\dfrac{4 - 1}{3} = 1$.\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx \\frac{1}{2}\\left[f(1)+ 2\\left(f(2) + f(3)\\right) + f(4) \\right]\\cdot 1\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $x$ and $f(x){:}$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $17$ | $14$ | $9$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\left[17 + 2\\left(14 + 9\\right)+ 2 \\right]\\cdot 1 = \\dfrac{65}{2}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave down* over the interval $[1,4]$, the trapezoids lie under the function, and therefore approximation is an *underestimate* of the correct area."] [17592186092077 :question/problem "For which of the graphs shown below would the trapezoidal sum give an underestimate of the area under the curve between $x=-2$ and $x=2$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/945/Source/Images/17dbb517c96a210f0b0f96e115c8a808.png)"] [17592186092077 :question/worked-solution "The trapezoidal rule underestimates the area under the curve if the function is concave down over the given interval.\n\nFrom the given graphs, the only curve that is concave down on $[-2,2]$ is the one shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/945/Source/Images/d74f9ebc9f6cc5895cd84281803d66ce.png)"] [17592186091953 :question/problem "Using the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n$[1,4.5] = [1,2] \\cup [2,4] \\cup [4,4.5]$,\napproximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $4.5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |"] [17592186091953 :question/worked-solution "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/945/Source/Images/a94ba1a4c5a782a263d6f3afca215ea2.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $\\mathcal{A}$:\n$1$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\nA \\mid \\approx \\frac{1 + 5}{2} \\cdot (2 - 1) + \\frac{5 + 7}{2} \\cdot (4 - 2) + \\frac{7 + 9}{2} \\cdot (4.5 - 4); = 3 \\cdot 1 + 6 \\cdot 2 + 8 \\cdot 0.5; = 3 + 12 + 4; = 19\n$$"] [17592186091954 :question/problem "The following table shows the speed of a car, in miles per hour, at certain moments in its $2$-hour journey.\n\n| $t \\left(\\textrm{hours}\\right)$ | $0$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v \\left(\\textrm{miles}/\\textrm{hour}\\right)$ | $0$ | $60$ | $40$ |\n\nUse the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n$[0,1.5] \\cup [1.5,2]$,\nto approximate the total distance traveled during the $2$-hour journey."] [17592186091954 :question/worked-solution "In this context, we have $n=2$ trapezoids, and the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the curve $y=v(t)$ represents the total distance traveled during the $2$-hour journey.\n\nUsing the trapezoidal rule, we estimate the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx \\dfrac{v(0)+v(1.5)}{2} \\cdot \\Delta t_1 + \\dfrac{v(1.5)+v(2)}{2} \\cdot \\Delta t_2\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\nA \\mid \\approx \\frac{0 + 60}{2} \\cdot (1.5 - 0) + \\frac{60 + 40}{2} \\cdot (2 - 1.5); = 30 \\cdot (1.5) + 50 \\cdot (0.5); = 70\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total distance covered during the $2$-hour journey is approximately $70\\,\\textrm{miles}$."]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
